// cg68k.cla: the `emit68k` skeleton -- native-5d Task 7. Turns the SAME
// arena IR (ir.cla) the C backend (cprint.cla) already consumes into a
// single asm68k.cla instruction stream: the native type-layout rules
// (cgSizeOf/cgAlignOf/cgFieldOffset), per-function JT-slot numbering
// (cgJtSlot), the caller/callee frame model (params above A6, locals
// below), a synthesized ROM-startup routine, and a string-literal constant
// pool. Every reachable function's own BODY is a placeholder comment this
// task ("TODO body Task 8") -- only the scaffolding around it (prologue,
// epilogue, frame layout, JT numbering) is real. cprint.cla is NOT included
// or depended on here: the C and 68k backends are siblings over the same
// IR, not layered on each other, so this file carries its own tiny
// toText/type-equality copies (cg68ToText/cg68TypeEqual) rather than reach
// into cprint.cla's.
//
// REAL MULTI-SEGMENT CONTAINER (Task 10 single-segment, Task 15
// generalizes to N). cg68Program's own output is one asm68k.cla stream
// PER SEGMENT (measure -> pack -> emit -- see the Task 15 section below
// for the full design); app68k.cla (included above) wraps all of them
// into a real MacBinary application image with one CODE resource per
// segment, written to outPath by cg68WriteImage -- unconditionally,
// whether or not --listing was passed. --listing ADDITIONALLY writes
// <base>.segN.s (a68ListingText()) and <base>.segN.dat (a68Bytes()) for
// EVERY segment N=1..cgSegCount, where <base> is outPath with its
// trailing ".ext" stripped ("out.bin" -> "out") -- a debug dump alongside
// the real image, not instead of it.
//
// LAYOUT AUTHORITY. cgSizeOf/cgAlignOf/cgFieldOffset are the ONLY place the
// native backend decides how big a Clarus type is or where a record field
// lives -- cprint.cla has no equivalent (C's own struct layout rules did
// that job there). The three follow one governing idea, spelled out once
// here: a scalar occupies its own byte size (4 for int/fixed/ptr/enum/
// text/list/map/winref/uistate/overlay/err; 1 for bool/char) but ANY time
// that scalar sits inside a record field, an array element, a frame local,
// or a frame param slot, it is padded up to 2 bytes (cgSlotSizeOf) -- the
// 68000 has no efficient odd-byte-addressed struct/stack story, so this
// backend never tries to pack two chars into one word. cgAlignOf follows
// directly from cgSizeOf (>=2 bytes needs to STA start on an even address;
// exactly 1 byte needs no alignment at all, though it will never actually
// appear unpadded once cgSlotSizeOf has run). str N is N+1 bytes (a Pascal
// length byte plus N content bytes) rounded up to even; arr N of T is N
// copies of T's own PADDED (slot) size; a record is its fields laid out in
// declaration order, each starting at its own alignment, rounded up to an
// even total. An overlay record's TYPE (KOverlay) is a bare 4-byte pointer
// (ir.cla's own doc comment: "a real pointer, unlike KRec's by-value
// struct"), but overlay fields themselves DO have real offsets --
// cgFieldOffset walks irRecords/irFieldSlots uniformly for both, since an
// overlay layout lives in the exact same two arenas a plain record's does
// (irRecordLayoutIsOverlay only changes whether a constructor gets
// generated, never the field chain shape). KWinRef/KUiState never occupy a
// record field or frame slot in any program this backend can reach yet (UI
// lowering is 5e territory) -- they get a definite 4-byte answer here
// anyway (same as every other pointer-shaped kind) so cgSizeOf stays a
// total function over all 17 IRKinds rather than a partial one no caller
// can safely invoke.
//
// SMALL-SCALAR-WIDTH PHASE (2026-08-05): bool/char occupy exactly 1 byte in
// every AGGREGATE -- ordinary record field, array element, extern record
// field -- on BOTH lanes. cgRecFieldSizeOf/cgRecFieldAlignOf remain the
// record-field-layout authority (cgRecordSize/cgFieldOffset/cgRecordCtorAt/
// cgEmitOneRcWalk all route through them, never cgSlotSizeOf directly for a
// field), but they no longer diverge from the packed (1-byte) size for
// bool/char -- see their own doc comment. cgArrElemStride packs bool
// alongside char for the identical reason. A bare LOCAL, PARAM, or GLOBAL
// scalar is unaffected by any of this: it still gets cgSlotSizeOf's
// ordinary 2-byte-padded slot (68000 alignment for locals/params is
// non-negotiable; see the FRAME MODEL section below). Historical note: an
// earlier rule (Task 14, native-5e fix-round) gave a bool/char RECORD FIELD
// a full 4-byte slot to match a since-fixed C runtime bug (a raw
// `*(int32_t *)` read over what was really a 1-byte field, the popuptable
// regression); that widened-field rule is gone as of this phase -- see
// `docs/superpowers/specs/2026-08-05-small-scalar-width-design.md` for the
// full rationale and survey.
//
// cprint.cla's C struct layout now largely AGREES with this scheme for
// bool/char (both pack at 1 byte in a record field or array element), but
// the two lanes remain independently computed -- cg68k.cla's own
// cgFieldOffset/cgRecordSize walk is still the ONLY layout authority this
// backend consults, and there is no requirement (or attempt) to keep every
// native offset numerically identical to cprint's C offsets for
// int/ptr/fixed fields, whose alignment/padding is decided by cg68k's own
// cgAlignOf here and by the m68k C compiler's own struct rules there. When
// Task 8+ builds the native serdesc tables (file save/load field-offset
// descriptors for the m68k path), those offsets MUST still come from
// cgFieldOffset, not assumed to match cprint's.
//
// FRAME MODEL, WORKED EXAMPLE. Params are pushed by the CALLER,
// left-to-right (a, then b, then c) before the JSR. Because the stack
// grows down, the LAST push ends up at the LOWEST address above the
// return-address/saved-A6 pair LINK sets up -- i.e. the last-DECLARED
// param sits at 8(A6), and earlier params sit at increasing offsets above
// it. For `func f(a: int, b: bool, c: int): int` (no hidden result
// pointer -- the 4-byte int return fits in D0): c is pushed last, so
// c@8(A6); cgSlotSizeOf(c=int)=4 advances to b@12(A6);
// cgSlotSizeOf(b=bool)=2 (padded) advances to a@14(A6). If the return
// type's cgSizeOf() were > 4, the caller pushes a hidden result pointer
// LAST of all (after c) -- it lands at 8(A6) and every real param shifts
// up by cgSlotSizeOf(ptr)=4 (c@12, b@16, a@18). Locals are the mirror
// image: declaration order, contiguous NEGATIVE offsets below A6, each
// slot cgSlotSizeOf(type) wide (a local frame is `LINK A6,#-frame` /
// `UNLK A6` bracketed, frame = the total negative extent). Locals dedup by
// (name, same type) exactly like cpEmitFunc (cprint.cla) does for its C
// hoist -- two sibling for-range loops sharing a bound-variable name is
// legal Clarus and gets ONE frame slot, reused; a genuine same-name/
// different-type collision is a hard error (abort() -- attempt-abort
// phase Task 6 converted this from `log(msg); quit 1`, non-UI-default
// observable behavior unchanged, design doc %3.5), mirroring cpEmitFunc's
// identical guard.
//
// JT SLOTS. Slot 0 is reserved for the synthesized startup routine (the
// first function emitted into the stream, per the brief) -- every
// REACHABLE (shakeReachable) irFuncs entry then gets the next slot, in
// plain declaration (irFuncs arena) order. Unreachable functions get no
// slot (cgJtSlot returns -1) and are never emitted at all, same skip
// cpEmitFuncs already applies on the C side.
//
// GLOBALS / A5. Every irGlobal gets a negative offset below A5, declaration
// order, contiguous, each slot cgSlotSizeOf(type) wide -- the exact same
// shape locals use below A6. The synthesized startup routine's first real
// work is zeroing this whole below-A5 block in one LEA+DBRA loop; a global
// with its own default (a record field default, or an explicit Init
// expression) is then overwritten by cg_init_globals (Task 12: real body,
// mirroring cpEmitGlobalsInit/cpDefaultInit -- see that function's own doc
// comment), called right after the zero loop.
//
// CONSTANT POOL. One string-literal entry per irStrLits slot (already
// populated by lower.cla regardless of which backend consumes it): a bound
// label, `DC.B <len>`, the raw content bytes, then an even-align. One
// value table per irEnums entry (Task 12: a plain DC.L list of that enum's
// member values, declaration order -- cgEmitEnumTables/cgEmitEnumConv).
// serdesc tables are declared-but-stubbed (a single comment marker) --
// real content is a later task (file.save/load field-offset descriptors).

include "lib.cla"
include "ir.cla"
include "shake.cla"
include "asm68k.cla"
include "app68k.cla"

// ================================================================
// Module state -- rebuilt fresh at the top of every cg68Program() call
// (mirrors shake.cla's shakeMarks/shakeWorklist "reassign, don't append"
// convention: a stale entry from a PRIOR compile in the same process would
// misalign against this compile's different irFuncs/irGlobals/irStrLits
// arenas).
// ================================================================

// cgJtSlots[i] is irFuncs index i's JT slot number (1-based; 0 is
// startup's own reserved slot), or -1 if i is unreachable (no slot, never
// emitted).
var cgJtSlots: list of int

// cgFuncLabels[i] is irFuncs index i's a68 label id (reserved up front, so
// a forward reference -- e.g. startup JSR-ing a handler emitted later in
// the stream -- can already resolve it), or -1 if i is unreachable.
var cgFuncLabels: list of int

// cgCbGlueFuncIdx[i]/cgCbGlueBodyFuncIdx[i] (Task 8, toolbox-integration
// Feature C: `callback func`) are parallel lists pairing each
// cg68SynthCbGlue-synthesized "clar_cb_<name>" glue IRFunc (its own
// irFuncs index) with the REAL `callback func` body IRFunc it wraps --
// cgCbGlueBodyFor's own lookup table, consulted by cgEmitFunc's by-name
// dispatch to route a glue func to cgEmitCallbackGlue instead of the
// ordinary LINK+cgStmts+UNLK body. Rebuilt fresh by every
// cg68SynthCbGlue call (this file's own irFuncs-index-parallel-array
// convention -- cgJtSlots/cgFuncLabels above never survive across
// compiles either).
var cgCbGlueFuncIdx: list of int
var cgCbGlueBodyFuncIdx: list of int

// cgGlobalOffsets[i] is irGlobals index i's negative A5 offset.
var cgGlobalOffsets: list of int

// cgGlobalOffByName maps irGlobalName(i) -> cgGlobalOffsets[i], keyed by
// interned nameIdx (findings 1.4, Task 13: cgFindGlobalOffset's own
// lookup table -- replaces a linear irGlobals scan with a poolGet PER
// CANDIDATE). Fresh-assigned by cgAssignGlobalOffsets, which runs
// exactly once per compile (cg68Measure's own "fresh-per-run" call), so
// no separate invalidation flag is needed the way cgRecIdxByNameValid
// needs one (that map is built lazily, on-demand, across multiple
// cg68Measure/cg68Program passes in the same compile).
var cgGlobalOffByName: intmap of int

// ================================================================
// Pre-interned runtime-call names (findings 1.4, Task 13)
// ================================================================

// rn* globals cache every runtime IRFunc name this backend calls BY
// NAME (cgCallRuntime/cgJsrByName/cgIntr1-4/cgStrBuilderIntoTemp/
// cgIntrUiForward/cgIntrFileSaveLoad/cgEmitContainerBirthCall/
// cgEmitPtrRcCall/cgEmitAtCall/cgEmitMapValAtCall/cgDeepReleaseContainer's
// own family-select locals/cgEmitPanic/cgEmitEnumCheckCall/
// cgEmitStoreErr, and every string-map "prefix + suffix" combo the
// map-hashtable phase's cgIntrMapSet/Get/GetDv/Remove/cgMapReleaseGuard/
// cgForMapStmt families build) -- pre-interned ONCE per process rather
// than re-interning the SAME literal on every one of the potentially
// thousands of call sites a real program's own codegen emits (findings
// 1.4: "every emitted runtime call... after interning a 256-byte
// literal"). rnInit()/rnInited is check.cla's own cnInit()/cnInited
// lazy-guard pattern (a global initializer cannot call intern() --
// see cnInit's own doc comment) -- called once, from cg68ProgramFork's
// own entry, before cg68Measure's first cgEmitFunc call needs any of
// these. NOT exhaustive by design: cgEmitStartupCallIfPresent's own
// literal call sites (rtUiStartup/rtUiLaunch/rtUiRun/natInit/the four
// handler_App_* names) fire at most once each, per COMPILE, not per
// call site emitted -- rare enough that pre-interning buys nothing
// measurable, so that one function is deliberately left calling
// intern() inline, same as cgCallExtUiGlueAddr/cgCallExtNatFallback's
// own genuinely program-dependent (not fixed-literal) names.
var rnInited: bool
var rnAlert: int
var rnFileName: int
var rnFileReadText: int
var rnFileWriteText: int
var rnLastErrCode: int
var rnLastErrMsg: int
